---
/**
 * The mechanism behind the claim every other page makes in one line.
 *
 * Every number on this page is derived from the manifest `zig build` emitted,
 * not written into the prose, for the same reason the footer version is: a
 * hand-maintained count of how many snippets are verified would be the one
 * figure on the site that nothing checks.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { buildInfo } from "../build-info";
import { snippetManifest } from "../snippets";
import { pageHref } from "../nav";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const { zigVersion } = await buildInfo();

const snippets = await snippetManifest();
const total = snippets.length;
const executables = snippets.filter((s) => s.kind === "exe").length;
const tests = snippets.filter((s) => s.kind === "test").length;
const inBrowser = snippets.filter((s) => s.runnable).length;
const mustNotCompile = snippets.filter((s) => s.compileFails).length;
const hostOnly = total - inBrowser - mustNotCompile;
const mustFail = snippets.filter((s) => s.expectFail).length;
---

<Base
  title="How this guide is verified"
  seoTitle="How Every Zig Snippet Here Is Verified Against Master"
  description="CI compiles and runs every snippet and diffs its output before the site can build, and again each night on a new Zig master. This is the mechanism."
  crumbs={[{ label: "Verification" }]}
  {/* `WebPage`, not `TechArticle`, and the distinction is load-bearing: the
      browser gate treats a `TechArticle` node as "this is a chapter" and then
      requires the git dates and the plain-Markdown twin that every chapter
      carries. This page is about the guide rather than in it, on the same
      footing as `/about/` and `/privacy/`. */}
  schema={{
    "@type": "WebPage",
    name: "How this guide is verified",
    author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Rajesh Pillai" },
  }}
>
  <article>
    <h1>How this guide is verified</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      Every page here claims that its code works. This page gives the
      mechanism behind that claim, with enough detail that you can check it
      yourself. CI compiles and runs all {total} snippets against Zig{" "}
      {zigVersion}. If one snippet fails, the site cannot be published.
    </p>

    <h2>What "verified" means</h2>
    <p>
      "Verified" means three separate things. Most documentation that claims
      to be tested does only the first.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>It compiles.</strong> The snippet compiles with the compiler
        that the footer names. That compiler is built from master, and does
        not come from a release.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>It runs.</strong> CI builds the binary and then runs it. If a
        snippet compiles and then panics on line one, that is a failure here.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Its output is what the chapter says.</strong> Most snippets have
        a sibling file that records their exact expected stdout, byte for byte.
        A compiler change can make a program print a different result, while
        the program still compiles and runs. That is a failure too. A smoke
        test does not find this case.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>One artifact, no second copy</h2>
    <p>
      The snippets are ordinary <code>.zig</code> files in the repository. This
      page does not contain a copy of the code that a person pasted in. When
      the site is built, the page reads the file from disk. Thus the listing in
      a chapter and the file that the compiler checked are the same bytes. The
      two cannot become different, because there is only one copy.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Run button works in the same way. When CI compiles a snippet, the
      result is a <code>.wasm</code> file. The verification step runs that
      file, and your browser downloads the same file. There is no second build
      for the web, and no server compiles code on demand. When you press Run,
      your browser does the check again, on your machine.
    </p>

    <h2>What the build finds by itself</h2>
    <p>
      No list configures the snippets. The build scans the snippet directory
      and reads each file to classify it. Thus, when you add a snippet, you
      cannot forget to register it. When you delete a snippet, no stale entry
      stays behind.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        If a file contains <code>pub fn main</code>, the build makes a program
        and captures its output. {executables} of the {total} are programs.
      </li>
      <li>
        The build makes each other file into a test binary, and runs it under
        the test runner. Thus its <code>expect</code> calls are real
        assertions. The other {tests} are tests.
      </li>
      <li>
        A sibling <code>.expected</code> file enables the comparison of the
        exact output. To enable it, add the file. You do not need to do more.
      </li>
      <li>
        If the name of a file starts with an underscore, the file is a helper
        module that other snippets import. It is not a snippet.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The {hostOnly} that cannot run in a browser</h2>
    <p>
      A browser tab has no sockets, and no threads under the WebAssembly System
      Interface. It has no real filesystem, only an empty directory in memory,
      and no window to draw into. A snippet that needs one of these has a
      marker. The build compiles that snippet for the architecture of the
      build machine, not for wasm, and runs it there during verification. On
      the page, the snippet shows as ordinary highlighted code. A note tells
      you why there is no Run button. If one command can run the snippet, the
      note also gives you that command.
    </p>
    <p>
      For a few snippets, the build compiles and links them, but never runs
      them. For example, the X11 chapter links against the real Xlib. A build
      that opened a window and waited for a keypress would stop the check
      forever. The compile and the link still find the problem that breaks
      these chapters: a change to the interface of the library.
    </p>
    <p>
      The protocol chapters in the networking section still have Run buttons.
      The reason is that the codec, the command parser and the connection
      handler take bytes and return bytes. They do not read a socket directly,
      so CI can verify them, and they can run anywhere. Only the chapters that
      teach the socket itself need a real socket.
    </p>

    <h2>The {mustFail} that are required to fail</h2>
    <p>
      Some chapters teach a runtime safety check, and the snippet makes the
      check fire. Examples are an out-of-bounds read and an integer overflow.
      For those snippets, the build turns safety checks on, and the
      verification is inverted. The build asserts that the program{" "}
      <em>stops</em>, with the exact message that a sibling file records. If
      the snippet exits successfully, or fails with different words, the build
      fails.
    </p>
    <p>
      Thus CI checks a panic message in a chapter every night. Nobody must
      remember it from the day it was written. The guide uses this marker
      rarely, and only where the check itself is the lesson. The reason is that
      a safety build is about fourteen times larger to download than a normal
      build.
    </p>
    <p>
      One rule tells what must never have this marker: a snippet whose
      important behaviour is undefined. Overflow is permitted, because a safety
      build promises a panic, and there is a correct message to pin. A read
      past the end of an array in a release build is not permitted. There is
      no correct answer, and a recorded expectation would assert undefined
      behaviour. Where this difference is important, the chapter states both
      halves, and shows only the defined half.
    </p>

    <h2>The {mustNotCompile} that are required not to compile</h2>
    <p>
      Some lessons are a compile error. Examples are a runtime value used as
      an array length, and a comptime loop that is longer than the branch
      quota. These snippets have a marker, and the build never makes a
      binary from them. The build compiles each one for wasm and asserts that
      the compiler stops. The compiler output must contain each line of a
      sibling file, in order.
    </p>
    <p>
      The page shows the lines of that file under the code. Thus the error
      message that you read is the message that CI checks. If the snippet
      starts to compile, or the compiler changes its words, the build fails.
    </p>

    <h2>What happens every night</h2>
    <p>
      At 06:00 UTC, CI gets a new Zig built from master, and runs the full
      chain against it. CI verifies every snippet and builds the site. Then CI
      tests the built site in a real headless browser. The site publishes again
      only if all of these steps pass.
    </p>
    <p>
      A check that runs when a person edits a page proves that the page was
      correct on the day of the edit. The nightly check proves that the page is
      correct today, on a compiler that did not exist when the chapter was
      written. When Zig changes something, the nightly check fails. The fix is
      to teach the new form, and to record what the old form was.
    </p>

    <h2>The browser check</h2>
    <p>
      The last gate loads the built site in headless Chromium, and acts like a
      reader. It presses Run on every playground and checks the output. It
      follows every internal link on every page, and fails on a link that does
      not resolve. It walks the full guide by its "next" arrows. Then it
      requires three things:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>The walk reaches every chapter.</li>
      <li>For every "next" arrow, the next page has the correct "previous" arrow.</li>
      <li>
        Exactly two pages have one arrow missing: the pages at the two ends.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The gate also measures things that are not visible on the page. These
      things could break, and nobody would see it:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The contrast of the text against its real composited background, in
        each palette and in light and dark mode, against the WCAG AA
        thresholds.
      </li>
      <li>
        On every page: a title, a description, one heading, one valid
        structured-data block, and a canonical URL that names the page itself.
      </li>
      <li>
        Every page is in the sitemap, and every sitemap entry resolves.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What none of this checks</h2>
    <p>
      The prose. A chapter can compile, run and print exactly the correct
      output, and still have a bad explanation. A chapter can also be out of
      date about something that the compiler does not check. Corrections are
      welcome, and the fastest route is the{" "}
      <a href={`${base}contact/`}>issue tracker</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two pages are outside the gate by design, and both pages tell you so. The
      chapter{" "}
      <a href={pageHref("getting-started/coming-from-older-zig")}
        >Migrate from an Older Zig</a> shows "before" examples that do not
      compile, on purpose, to show what changed. The chapter on{" "}
      <a href={pageHref("getting-started/zig-0-17")}>what changed in Zig 0.17</a>
      shows 0.16.0 code and error messages. A person checked them by hand
      against both compilers, because CI has only the current compiler.
    </p>

    <p>
      The build that does all of this is public, at{" "}
      <a
        href="https://github.com/rajeshpillai/zig-guide"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">github.com/rajeshpillai/zig-guide</a>. See
      also <a href={`${base}about/`}>about this guide</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
